- **DECLARED** `section-tag` [dark] (.sc-ability__section-head .tag → .dse-section__title): font-size miss: site 18px, plugin 15px (tol 1.5px)
  - _declared:_ SC-235 -- DELIBERATE divergence, option B (Scott's ruling, comment 00d1a795, 2026-09-28). The site's Petrona has no small-caps glyphs at all, so the browser SYNTHESIZES small caps by shrinking capitals to 70% (its computed 18px renders as 8px of ink); the plugin's Source Serif 4 Bold has REAL smcp glyphs, which render taller per unit font-size than a synthesized cap does. Option A (18px, matching the site's COMPUTED value) put real smcp glyphs at a size chosen for fake ones, rendering about 25% taller than the site. 15px of real smcp renders the site's own 8px of ink -- this pair's computed font-size now targets the RENDERED match, not the computed one, and 15px vs the site's 18px is the deliberate, measured consequence, not a miss to close.
- **DECLARED** `section-tag` [dark] (.sc-ability__section-head .tag → .dse-section__title): line-height miss: site 30.6px, plugin 25.5px (tol 1.5px)
  - _declared:_ SC-235 -- a pure CONSEQUENCE of the :font-size entry above, not an independent miss. Both sides run the same 1.7 ratio (site 1.7 x 18px = 30.6px; plugin 1.7 x 15px = 25.5px, inherited from the Plan 21 card-root rule). It is listed separately only because the gate reports per rule.
- **DECLARED** `pr-chars` [dark] (.sc-ability__pr-head .chars → .dse-pr__head): ink miss: site color=rgb(205, 209, 212), plugin color=rgba(220, 226, 230, 0.95) (max channel 18.000 > 2 FIRES; alpha 0.050 > 0.03 FIRES)
  - _declared:_ FOLLOWUPS #40 / SC-110 -- DELIBERATE divergence, not a measurement artifact. The site splits its power-roll caption into .pre (dim metal, 'Power Roll +') and .chars (the bright roll readout); the plugin's .dse-pr__head is ONE node carrying the whole caption, painted at var(--dse-heading) so the line reads as a heading. That is brighter than the site's .chars in dark (220,226,230 @.95 vs 205,209,212) and darker in light (26,29,32 vs 95,104,109) -- more emphasis, both schemes, by design. Matching .chars exactly would de-emphasise the plugin's only power-roll heading. Changing it is a PIXEL decision for Scott, not a CSS catch-up.
